findings.fix:
Copy the streaming pattern the pre-update backup already uses in src/services/updateService.ts:
const dump = spawn('pg_dump', ['--no-owner','--no-acl','--clean','--if-exists'], { env: { ...process.env, PGPASSWORD, PGHOST, PGPORT, PGUSER, PGDATABASE } });
await pipeline(dump.stdout, createGzip(), cipherStream, createWriteStream(backupPath));
Use createCipheriv as a Transform in the pipeline instead of buffering, then stream the finished file to the client with createReadStream(backupPath).pipe(res). Drop the fixed 120 s timeout in favour of a no-output watchdog, and report progress through the existing status endpoint rather than holding the HTTP request open.

Envelope-encrypt the secret fields rather than the whole row. Keep the existing secretFieldsFor() map as the field list, add a small helper that seals and opens values with AES-256-GCM under a key that never lives in the database, and call it at the service seam so route code is unchanged:
const sealed = seal(config.password);   // { v:1, iv, tag, ct }
const plain  = open(sealed);
Source the key from Azure Key Vault (or, as an interim step, a POLARIS_SECRET_KEY env var delivered the same way SESSION_SECRET is) so a database dump on its own is inert. Ship a one-shot startup job that seals existing rows in place, keyed on the absence of the version marker, exactly like the other backfill jobs in src/jobs/. Separately, make the backup passphrase mandatory while the data is still plaintext.

Two layers. First, make the invariant enforceable in the database. A unique index kills exact duplicates immediately and needs no extension:
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX subnets_block_cidr_key ON subnets ("blockId", cidr);
For true overlap, add btree_gist and an exclusion constraint on the inet range:
CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS btree_gist;
ALTER TABLE subnets ADD CONSTRAINT subnets_no_overlap
  EXCLUDE USING gist ("blockId" WITH =, inet(cidr) inet_ops WITH &&);
Second, wrap the read plus write in one transaction and serialize per block so the check cannot be raced:
await prisma.$transaction(async (tx) => {
  await tx.$executeRaw`SELECT pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtext(${blockId}))`;
  // re-read siblings, test overlap, create
});
Translate the resulting P2002 or 23P01 into the existing 409 AppError so the API contract does not change.

Either enforce the documented floor or delete the claim. To enforce it, gate the mount the same way /utilization and /map/regions already are:
router.use("/map", requirePermission("deviceMap", "read"), mapRouter);
and add `perm: ["deviceMap", "read"]` to the Device Map entry in NAV_ITEMS so the sidebar matches. tests/integration/topologyLayout.test.ts currently asserts the reads work for a deviceMap=read caller, which stays true; add a case for a deviceMap=none role expecting 403.

Take the same per-block advisory lock as the TXN-01 fix as the first statement inside the existing transaction, so the two paths serialize against each other as well as against themselves:
const result = await prisma.$transaction(async (tx) => {
  await tx.$executeRaw`SELECT pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtext(${input.blockId}))`;
  const existing = await tx.subnet.findMany({ where: { blockId: input.blockId }, select: { cidr: true } });
  ...
});
The exclusion constraint from TXN-01 is the backstop if the lock is ever bypassed.

Stop putting the URL on the command line. Parse it once and pass the parts through the environment, which pg_dump and psql both read:
const u = new URL(connUrl);
const env = { ...process.env, PGHOST: u.hostname, PGPORT: u.port || '5432', PGUSER: decodeURIComponent(u.username), PGPASSWORD: decodeURIComponent(u.password), PGDATABASE: u.pathname.slice(1) };
spawn('pg_dump', ['--no-owner','--no-acl','--clean','--if-exists'], { env });
And never echo a child-process error message to the client: log err.message server-side through pino and return a fixed string, for example `throw new AppError(500, "pg_dump failed; see server logs")`.

Make the missing backup a decision the operator takes deliberately rather than one the code takes for them. Mark the step failed and stop by default:
} catch (err: any) {
  failUpdate(0, "Pre-update backup failed: " + (err.message || "pg_dump not available"));
  return;
}
and add an explicit `allowWithoutBackup: true` flag on POST /server-settings/updates/apply, surfaced in the UI as a checkbox that has to be ticked, for the case where the operator genuinely wants to proceed. If a soft path is kept, use a distinct step state so it does not render green.

Bound the concurrency and batch the writes, using the mapWithConcurrency helper the agent installer already uses:
const results = await mapWithConcurrency(needsPrimary, 20, async (asset) => { ... return { id, data }; });
await prisma.$transaction(results.map(r => prisma.asset.update({ where: { id: r.id }, data: r.data })));
Chunk the transaction at a few hundred statements. Better still, move the sweep behind the existing job machinery in src/jobs/ and have the route return 202 with a run id the UI polls, which is how discovery and bulk agent install already behave.

Cap the batch and bound the fan-out. Add `.max(500)` to both id arrays so an oversized request is a clean 400 rather than a hung one, and replace the serial loop with the bounded pool already used for bulk agent install:
const results = await mapWithConcurrency(input.ids, 8, async (id) => { try { ... } catch (err) { return { id, ok: false, message: err.message }; } });
Keep the per-item ok/message result shape, which is already correct. If a larger batch has to be supported, return 202 and drain it through a job the way bulk-agent-install does.

Add a handler in src/index.ts before anything else boots, so every role inherits it:
process.on("unhandledRejection", (reason) => {
  logger.fatal({ err: reason, role: process.env.POLARIS_ROLE ?? "all" }, "unhandled promise rejection");
  process.exit(1);
});
process.on("uncaughtException", (err) => {
  logger.fatal({ err, role: process.env.POLARIS_ROLE ?? "all" }, "uncaught exception");
  process.exit(1);
});
Exit rather than continue, so systemd still restarts into a known state, but the structured log now names the origin. Pair it with a `polaris_process_crash_total{role}` counter so a restart loop is visible in Grafana instead of only in journald.

Use the shared helper in all three handlers:
import { requestActor } from "../middleware/auth.js";
...
await acceptConflict(conflict, requestActor(req));
It returns the session username for browser callers and `api:<token name>` for bearer callers, so token-driven resolutions become traceable to the token.

Ask the filesystem instead:
const sizeBytes = existsSync(backupFile) ? statSync(backupFile).size : 0;
statSync is already imported in the module's sibling utilities and the call is O(1).
